From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:08:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(pdu,graphics): read Progressive bitmap streams one block
 at a time

The Progressive decoder collected a bitmap's whole stream before
decoding any of it: every block, and every tile block of every REGION
block, including the REGION blocks it then skipped for being outside the
stream's first frame. Collected, a tile block takes 60 bytes in the
WebAssembly build (112 on 64-bit targets) when it can take 22 on the
wire, and a FRAME_END block 52 (104) for 6, so the limit on the tiles a
bitmap decodes came too late: a server could make the client hold
several times a bitmap's size. On 64-bit, eight REGION blocks of 65535
empty tile blocks (11 MiB) took 56 MiB, and a million FRAME_END blocks
(6 MB) took 156 MiB. A 256 MiB stream of FRAME_END blocks, the most ZGFX
lets one PDU decompress to, grew the vector of its blocks to 3.25 GiB in
the WebAssembly build, out of the 4 GiB the instance every session
shares can have.

ironrdp-pdu's new decode_progressive_stream_blocks iterates over a
stream's blocks, decoding one at a time, and hands each REGION block's
tile blocks to an iterator that decodes one at a time too. decode_bitmap
reads the stream with it twice: whole first, so that a malformed stream
still fails before any state changes, and then for the REGION blocks of
its first frame. It also refuses a first frame of more tile blocks than
MAX_TILES_PER_BITMAP before decoding any, where it failed only after
decoding that many. The streams above now take under a kilobyte.
decode_progressive_stream still collects a stream, but no longer
reserves room for the tile blocks a REGION block's header claims before
reading them.
